# Ignore everything without an extension, then selectively allow files and directories.
*
!*.*
!*/

# Don't ignore makefiles and license files.
!makefile
!Makefile
!LICENSE
!LICENSE-CONTENT

# Nor the two submodules. A submodule is a gitlink rather than a directory as far as the index is
# concerned, and neither path has an extension, so the `*` above would ignore both and
# `git submodule add` would refuse them.
!libs/test
!tools/avrsim

# Ignore the AVR toolchain's output. The rules above re-include anything with an extension, so
# every generated file type has to be named here explicitly.
*.elf
*.hex
*.eep
*.bin
*.map
*.lst
*.obj
*.o
*.d
*.a
*.su

# Ignore the Python environment and bytecode used to redraw the diagrams.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Ignore the book's intermediate output: LuaLaTeX's aux, log and toc files and the figure PDFs,
# all regenerated by `make` in book/. The finished book/embedded-asm.pdf is not under this rule.
book/build/

# Ignore IDE and editor directories.
.vscode/
.vs/
.idea/

# Ignore temp/ and scratch trees.
temp/
scratch/

# Ignore the implementation code, keeping the interfaces, stubs, tests and tools we ship.
#
# This course hands out contracts and tests, never bodies. The AVR subroutines under
# drivers/source and the application program under drivers/app are the reader's to write.
# Solving an exercise leaves that work sitting in the working tree where a single `git add -A`
# would publish it, so the lines below make "not pushed" structural rather than a thing to
# remember.
#
# Note that a gitignore `*` never crosses a `/`, so each of these is exactly one level deep and
# needs no re-include. Already-tracked files are unaffected, so every README.md and every .inc
# contract we ship inside these directories stays tracked either way. If some future lecture
# genuinely needs to ship a body, `git add -f` overrides this.
drivers/source/*.asm
drivers/app/*.asm

# L06 alone adds two more kinds of reader-written file in the same two directories: a GNU `as`
# translation beside the AVRASM2 original, and the C program that calls it. Both are answers to
# the capstone and both stay out of the repository for the same reason as the assembly.
drivers/source/*.S
drivers/app/*.S
drivers/source/*.c
drivers/app/*.c

# The top-level units ci/build.sh generates, and everything else it puts beside them.
drivers/build/

# The worked solutions, which go out after their lecture rather than with it. Each one is
# committed with `git add -f` on the day its lecture is given; until then it sits here in the
# working tree, where the author can write it and `ci/links.sh` can check it, without a stray
# `git add -A` publishing an answer to an exercise nobody has attempted yet.
#
# ci/check.sh knows about this: a lecture whose last appendix is the exercises is correct and
# says "solutions not published", and one that ends with the solutions says so too.
#
# The exam papers' solutions are deliberately not here. They answer a paper taken after the
# whole course rather than after a lecture, and exam/README.md is built around their being
# available from the start.
lectures/*/appendix/*_solutions.md

# Ignore a reference implementation kept locally for validating the shipped test suites. Writing
# one, running the suites against it and deleting it is how a suite gets checked before it goes
# out; keeping it out of the repo is what stops it becoming the published answer.
solutions/
